  "requestTimestamp": "2026-07-19T05:49:05.832Z",
  "answer": "### Short Answer\nAn individual earning purely compensation income from a single employer in the Philippines is not required to file an annual income tax return if the employer has correctly withheld the tax.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\nThis is governed by Section 51-A of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC), which states that individual taxpayers receiving purely compensation income from only one employer, with correct tax withholding, are exempt from filing an annual income tax return.\n\n### Interpretation\nSection 51-A of the NIRC explicitly provides that employees whose income tax has been correctly withheld by their employer are not required to file a return. The certificate of withholding issued by the employer, stamped as received by the Bureau of Internal Revenue (BIR), serves as a substitute for the income tax return.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nThis provision simplifies the tax compliance process for employees with straightforward compensation income scenarios, reducing the administrative burden on both taxpayers and the BIR. It allows individuals to rely on their employer's withholding as sufficient documentation of their tax obligations, provided that the withholding is accurate and properly reported.",
